mcqs.question:
A chat list keeps the selection in parent state. The header counter confirms that `selectedId` changes on every tap, but no row is ever highlighted, and `messages` keeps the same array reference:

```tsx
function MessageList({ messages, selectedId, onSelect }) {
  const renderItem = useCallback(
    ({ item }: { item: Message }) => (
      <Row item={item} selected={item.id === selectedId} onPress={() => onSelect(item.id)} />
    ),
    [],
  );

  return <FlatList data={messages} renderItem={renderItem} keyExtractor={(item) => item.id} />;
}
```

What is the defect, and why does the usual `extraData` advice not repair it?

A web component is ported almost verbatim and throws `ReferenceError: document is not defined` on its first render, plus a warning that `<div>` is not a valid component:

```tsx
function Card({ title }: { title: string }) {
  const root = document.getElementById('card-root');
  return <div style={{ display: 'grid', padding: '1rem' }}>{title}</div>;
}
```

Which statement describes what React Native actually renders?

code.code:
const ROW_HEIGHT = 64;

const RowBase = ({ message, selected, onSelect }: RowProps) => (
  <Pressable onPress={() => onSelect(message.id)} style={[styles.row, selected && styles.rowSelected]}>
    <Text>{message.body}</Text>
  </Pressable>
);

const Row = memo(RowBase, (prev, next) =>
  prev.message === next.message && prev.onSelect === next.onSelect,
);

export function MessageList({ messages, selectedId, onSelect }: Props) {
  const renderItem = useCallback(
    ({ item }: { item: Message }) => (
      <Row message={item} selected={item.id === selectedId} onSelect={onSelect} />
    ),
    [selectedId, onSelect],
  );

  return (
    <FlatList
      data={messages}
      renderItem={renderItem}
      keyExtractor={(item) => item.id}
      getItemLayout={(_, index) => ({ length: ROW_HEIGHT, offset: ROW_HEIGHT * index, index })}
      initialNumToRender={10}
    />
  );
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
**Bug 1 is a `memo` comparator that ignores the prop that changes.** `FlatList` is not the culprit here: `renderItem` is a `useCallback` whose dependencies include `selectedId`, so every selection change produces a new function, the list re-renders, and each `Row` element is rebuilt with a fresh `selected` boolean. The bailout is one level down. The custom comparator in `memo` only compares `message` and `onSelect`; both are referentially identical across a tap for every row, so it returns `true` and React keeps the previous element - including its old `selected` prop and its old style. That also explains the second half of the symptom: editing a message replaces its object, `prev.message !== next.message` becomes true, the row finally re-renders, and the highlight shows up on the row that was already selected.

The fix is the comparator contract, and `extraData` is not part of it. Adding `extraData={selectedId}` here would be harmless but redundant, because `renderItem` already changes identity on selection. `extraData` is the marker for the opposite case: a `renderItem` whose reference stays stable while values outside `data` affect the output. The list only skips work when its own props are shallow-equal, and here `renderItem` already changed (https://reactnative.dev/docs/flatlist).

```tsx
// preferred: let the default shallow comparison do the work
const Row = memo(RowBase);

// if a custom comparator has to stay, compare every prop the row renders from
const RowWithComparator = memo(RowBase, (prev, next) =>
  prev.message === next.message &&
  prev.onSelect === next.onSelect &&
  prev.selected === next.selected,
);
```

Keeping `onSelect` stable and letting the row call `onSelect(message.id)` is what makes that comparison possible at all; an inline `onPress={() => onSelect(item.id)}` recreated per render would make the comparator useless and re-render every row. That is probably why the earlier performance pass introduced the comparator, and the pass simply forgot the prop that changes most.

**Bug 2 is `getItemLayout` lying about geometry.** `getItemLayout` exists so the list can skip measuring by trusting the declared length and offset, and it is only correct for fixed-size items (https://reactnative.dev/docs/flatlist). Bodies wrap to two or three lines, so real heights range from roughly 64 to 150 px while the list still computes offsets at 64 px per row. Every offset after the first wrapped row drifts, the rendered window is computed from wrong positions - which is what the blank bands during a fast scroll are - and `scrollToIndex` targets a computed offset that no longer corresponds to the message, landing a few rows away. There are two honest fixes, and they are a product decision:

- delete `getItemLayout` and let the list measure variable-height rows (simplest, keeps the full text);
- or make the height true: clamp the body with `numberOfLines={2}` and a fixed row height, and derive both the style and `getItemLayout` from the same `ROW_HEIGHT` constant.

```tsx
const Row = memo(RowBase);

export function MessageList({ messages, selectedId, onSelect }: Props) {
  const renderItem = useCallback(
    ({ item }: { item: Message }) => (
      <Row message={item} selected={item.id === selectedId} onSelect={onSelect} />
    ),
    [selectedId, onSelect],
  );

  return (
    <FlatList
      data={messages}
      renderItem={renderItem}
      keyExtractor={(item) => item.id}
      // no getItemLayout: the row height depends on the wrapped body
    />
  );
}
```

Verification, because both bugs are visual and easy to declare fixed: on a representative low-end Android device with a release-like build, scroll fast through a long thread and watch for blank bands, `scrollToIndex` to a message whose body wraps three lines, and count `Row` renders while tapping - only the two rows whose selection changed should render.

code.code:
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';
import { Animated, StyleSheet, View } from 'react-native';

export function ProgressBar({ progress }: { progress: number }) {
  const value = useRef(new Animated.Value(0)).current;
  const [percent, setPercent] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    value.addListener(({ value: v }) => setPercent(Math.round(v * 100)));
    Animated.timing(value, {
      toValue: progress,
      duration: 250,
      useNativeDriver: true,
    }).start();
  }, [progress]);

  const width = value.interpolate({
    inputRange: [0, 1],
    outputRange: ['0%', '100%'],
  });

  return (
    <View style={styles.track} accessibilityValue={{ now: percent, min: 0, max: 100 }}>
      <Animated.View style={[styles.fill, { width }]} />
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  track: { height: 8, borderRadius: 4, backgroundColor: '#e5e7eb', overflow: 'hidden' },
  fill: { height: '100%', backgroundColor: '#2563eb' },
});

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Prediction: the change fails at start, not per frame. `width` is a layout property, and the native driver serializes the animation and runs it without the JS thread and without driving layout, so React Native rejects the style property before the first frame. That means `false` was not the bug and `true` is not the fix: with the flag off the bar moves but stutters, with it on the bar does not move at all.

The stutter has a different cause, and it is independent of the driver. `value.addListener(...)` fires on every animated frame and calls `setPercent`, so each frame schedules a React render on the JS thread. While that thread is busy - parsing a response, running a sync, a large commit - the callbacks queue and the bar updates late, which is exactly the "stutters when JS is busy" report. The listener also has no cleanup, so an unmount mid-animation leaves it attached and can call `setState` on a dead component.

A third, quieter defect: `width` is produced by interpolating the value into percentage strings. Interpolation on a supported property is fine, but here it feeds a layout prop, so it does not make the animation native-drivable; it only makes the failure less obvious when the driver is off.

The rewrite animates a non-layout property with the native driver, and keeps the JS thread out of the frame path:

```tsx
import { useEffect, useRef } from 'react';
import { Animated, StyleSheet, View } from 'react-native';

export function ProgressBar({ progress }: { progress: number }) {
  const scale = useRef(new Animated.Value(0)).current;

  useEffect(() => {
    const animation = Animated.timing(scale, {
      toValue: progress,
      duration: 250,
      useNativeDriver: true,
    });
    animation.start();
    return () => animation.stop();
  }, [progress, scale]);

  return (
    <View
      style={styles.track}
      accessibilityRole="progressbar"
      accessibilityValue={{ now: Math.round(progress * 100), min: 0, max: 100 }}
    >
      <Animated.View
        style={[styles.fill, { transform: [{ scaleX: scale }], transformOrigin: 'left' }]}
      />
    </View>
  );
}
```

`scaleX` from 0 to 1 stretches the full-width fill from the left edge (`transformOrigin` on the left; on older versions, compensate with a `translateX` of half the width on the parent). The native driver keeps it running while JS is blocked, and the per-frame `setState` disappears: the accessibility percentage is derived from the `progress` prop, not mirrored from an animated value.

If the design genuinely needs the width itself to animate - for example to clip siblings - the same contract moves to a UI-thread style library: a shared value updated with `withTiming`, read inside `useAnimatedStyle`, so the style is produced on the UI thread; keep the accessibility number and any business state on JS, committed at the start and the end, never per frame.

What to verify: block the JS thread deliberately (a long synchronous task, or pause in the debugger) and confirm the bar still animates; trace on a physical device with a release-like build and check the bar produces no per-frame JS work; and keep one driver per animated value, because mixing a native-driver and a JS-driver animation on the same `Animated.Value` is not supported.

