import { NavigationContainer } from '@react-navigation/native';

const linking = {
  prefixes: ['example://'],
  config: {
    screens: {
      Feed: 'feed',
      Profile: 'users/:id',
    },
  },
};

export function App() {
  return (
    <NavigationContainer linking={linking} fallback={<Text>Loading...</Text>}>
      <RootStack.Navigator initialRouteName="Home">
        <RootStack.Screen name="Home" component={HomeTabs} />
        <RootStack.Screen name="Profile" component={ProfileScreen} />
        <RootStack.Screen name="NotFound" component={NotFoundScreen} />
      </RootStack.Navigator>
    </NavigationContainer>
  );
}

function HomeTabs() {
  return (
    <Tabs.Navigator>
      <Tabs.Screen name="Feed" component={FeedScreen} />
      <Tabs.Screen name="Settings" component={SettingsScreen} />
    </Tabs.Navigator>
  );
}

function ProfileScreen({ route }: { route: { params: { id: string } } }) {
  const { data } = useQuery(['user', route.params.id], () =>
    fetchUser(route.params.id),
  );
  return <Profile user={data} />;
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Two different layers are broken, and they fail for unrelated reasons.

**The custom scheme lands on Home because the config does not match the navigator tree.** `Feed` is not a root screen: it lives inside the `Home` tab navigator. React Navigation matches `config.screens` against the nested structure, so a root-level `Feed: 'feed'` entry matches nothing, the path stays unhandled, and the container renders its initial route. The same mismatch breaks the reverse direction, where `<Link>` and `useLinkTo` generate paths that no screen owns. Any nesting has to be reflected in the config (https://reactnavigation.org/docs/configuring-links):

```tsx
const linking = {
  prefixes: ['example://', 'https://app.example.com'],
  config: {
    screens: {
      Home: { screens: { Feed: 'feed' } },
      Profile: 'users/:id',
      NotFound: '*',
    },
  },
};
```

Adding `NotFound: '*'` matters for operability: without a wildcard, an unmatched URL silently looks like success and lands wherever the initial route is, which is why this bug was reported as "opens on Home" rather than "fails".

**The https link opens the browser because the OS never associated the app with the domain, and the prefix was missing too.** These are separate gaps, and fixing the JS prefix alone does not close the second. The `linking` prop only parses URLs that the operating system has already handed to the app; it cannot claim a domain. On iOS the association is the Associated Domains entitlement (`applinks:app.example.com`) plus an `apple-app-site-association` file served by the domain. On Android it is an `autoVerify` VIEW intent filter plus a Digital Asset Links file. They are configured separately, and both are decided before any JavaScript runs (https://reactnavigation.org/docs/deep-linking). The `https://app.example.com` prefix still belongs in `prefixes`, otherwise the app opens and then cannot map the URL to a screen.

**The route parameter is untrusted input.** `users/:id` comes from a string anyone can craft, so validate it against the expected shape before fetching, and type the parameter list so `Profile` cannot render without an `id`. Fetching in the screen body before validation turns a malformed link into a 404 loop or, worse, into a value that is forwarded to a backend unchecked.

**Keep a single owner for both entry points.** `NavigationContainer` with `linking` already handles the cold start (initial URL) and the running app (URL events). A second manual `Linking.addEventListener` that calls `navigate` competes with it and can navigate twice; if push notifications also deep link, use the `getInitialURL` and `subscribe` overrides instead of a parallel listener.

**Verification and the product limit.** Test with `npx uri-scheme open "example://feed" --ios` and `--android`, then a real cold start (kill the app, open the link), then the https link from another app such as Notes, Messages or Gmail - not from the address bar, which does not trigger universal links. Also state the limit up front: a Universal or App Link opens the app only if it is already installed, and a user who installs afterwards normally loses the original URL context, so the store-redirect path needs its own deferred-link strategy.
